'''archegonium''' (är-kẹ̄-gō′ni-um), ''n.''; pl. ''archegonia'' (-ạ̈). [NL., < Gr. ἀρχέγονος, first of a race, original, < ἀρχε-, ἀρχι-, first (see ''archi-''), + γόνος, race: see ''-gony''.] The pistillidium or female organ of the higher cryptogams, having the same function as the pistil in flowering plants. {{smaller|It is a cellular sac, containing at the bottom a cell, analogous to the embryo-sac of phænogamous plants, which is impregnated by spermatozoöids from the male organ (antheridium). From this, after fertilization, the new plant is produced directly, as in the ferns and their allies, or a spore-case is developed, as in the mosses, when new plants follow upon the germination of the spores.}}

'''archegony''' (är-keg′ọ̄-ni), ''n.'' [< Gr. as if *ἀρχεγονία, < ἀρχέγονος, first of a race: see ''archegonium''.] The doctrine of the origin of life; specifically, the doctrine of spontaneous generation; archebiosis; abiogenesis.
{{smaller block|He [Haeckel] considers that, though the doctrine of spontaneous generation (or ''archegony'') has not been proved, it is quite possible, and even probable, the arguments against it resting on merely negative results.
{{right|''The Scotsman'' (newspaper).}}}}

'''Archegosauria''' (är″kẹ̄-gọ̄-sâ′ri-ạ̈), ''n. pl.'' [NL.: see ''Archegosaurus''.] A suborder or other group of extinct labyrinthodont amphibians, typified by the genus ''Archegosaurus''. The name is a loose synonym of ''Labyrinthodontia''.

